#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054381 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4007f63e-0a99-57ca-a1f6-190b21f02ecc" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Equations Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "c64cd16e-2f9d-5564-8d04-eddcad2c5d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-12859" :question/problem "Find the value of $q$ that satisfies the equation $\\dfrac{q}{4}=-9$." :question/worked-solution "In the equation, $q$ is being divided by $4$. To isolate $q$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $4$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4$.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{q}{4} &=-9 \\\\[5pt] 4\\cdot \\dfrac{q}{4} &=4\\cdot \\left(-9\\right) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{4}q}{\\cancel{4}} &= -36 \\\\ q &=-36 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054382 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "712bc4c6-aeaf-5081-9e64-791027b38963" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Equations Requiring Multiplication by a Negative Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "5fe35108-2fa8-563e-862e-ad27d4ed5334" :question/math-academy-id "e-12860" :question/problem "Find the value of $k$ that satisfies the equation $-\\dfrac{k}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{k}{2} &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-\\frac{k}{2}) &= (-1) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{k}{2} &= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $k$ is being divided by $2$. To isolate $k$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{k}{2} &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[5pt] 2 \\cdot \\dfrac{k}{2} &=2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{2k}{2} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}k}{\\cancel{2}} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt] k &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e153c18d-6f3e-5fef-a006-c5597cd88bc9" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Equations Requiring Division"}] :question/id #uuid "68865bc2-e4e2-53d9-9988-4fa962e04746" :question/math-academy-id "e-12861" :question/problem "Solve the equation $24=-3z$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the equation in a way that's a little more familiar to us. We can do this by swapping the left and right-hand sides of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24 &= -3z \\\\\n- 3z &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-3$. To isolate $z$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-3$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} -3z &=24 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {-3z} {-3} &= \\dfrac {24} {-3} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {\\cancel{-3}z} {\\cancel{-3}} &= -8 \\\\[5pt] z &=-8 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b765f7b2-639a-5519-9170-02a4fcb0f8fe" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Variable Is on the Right-Hand Side"}] :question/id #uuid "cbce1708-81ba-5d37-ae21-fc8d94ebbc2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12894" :question/problem "If $\\dfrac{1}{7}=\\dfrac{x}{2}$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $x$ is being divided by $2$. To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{2} &= \\frac{1}{7} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} &= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{2}{7} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{2}{7} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{2}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
